# Generated from analysis/generated/symbols.json for pinned MesenCE.
# Native syntax: MemoryType:HEX_ADDRESS:Label[:Comment].
SnesPrgRom:0584:Interrupt_LongVector73:Exact `JMP [$0073]`. The only explicit complete 24-bit construction found in banks 80-83 is `82:D73B-D742`, which sets `$73:$75 = 80:85A4` while interrupts are disabled during interrupt/HDMA setup.
SnesPrgRom:0599:Interrupt_LongVector53:Exact `JMP [$0053]`. Complete paired assignments set `$53:$55` to `80:F60C`, `80:8610`, or `80:85A5`; the same vector is invoked by wrapper `80:B0DE`. See `analysis/generated/remaining-indirect-vector-map.md`.
SnesPrgRom:0881:Main_StateDispatchLoop:Repeated top-level loop. After housekeeping it indexes state `$9B`, loads one of six handler addresses from the word table at `80:88B2`, pushes synthetic return `80:88AD` with `PER`, and `JMP ($005D)`s to the selected mode handler. Returning handlers land at `80:88AE` and branch back to `80:8881`.
SnesPrgRom:0C41:Text_TestCharacterMetadataBit7:Shared helper reached from four text/layout loops including `03:8998`, `00:C4CB`, and `00:C510`. It indexes the byte table at `00:C6F8` by character code and tests bit 7. Printable-table inspection shows uppercase A-Z in the low half while lowercase a-z, digits and much punctuation usually carry bit 7; therefore this is a verified character-metadata class test, not yet a proven glyph-width flag. Structural equivalents: Europe retail `80:8C41` (supported, 84.6% byte similarity) and PAL prototype `80:8C3C` (strong, 92.3%).
SnesPrgRom:11D1:Reset_Entry:Reset vector `80:8858` jumps here. The routine executes `CLC; XCE`, establishes 8-bit A / 16-bit index mode, disables interrupts, then tail-jumps to `80:91DC` for stack/direct-page, PPU/CPU register and WRAM initialization before entering the normal startup chain.
SnesPrgRom:14EB:Fatal_GamePakNotFound:Failure target of `CartridgeMemorySelfTest`. Its stream at `80:9505` uses resolved frontend controls `F9/FC/FF` around the literal `game_pak_not_found`, proving normal return from the `80:C3AB` call on this path.
SnesPrgRom:43AB:Frontend_TextCommandStream:Nitrodon bank-80 disassembly shows a byte-command parser with control bytes `>= 0xEE`, text/layout handlers, and returns through `80:C440`; bounded historical replay tracing executes this interpreter during frontend transition construction.
SnesPrgRom:43C8:Frontend_TextCommandDispatch:Exact instruction `JMP ($005D)`. Canonical-ROM decoding maps valid commands `FF..EF` to 17 concrete bank-80 handlers at `80:C3CB`; run `36795810324` proves live execution. `EE` is a boundary escape/sentinel whose computed next word is not a valid ROM handler, leaving only a range-proof invariant rather than unknown targets. See `analysis/generated/frontend-text-command-dispatch.md`.
SnesPrgRom:8050:Race_HandleCheckpointFinish:Runtime course-object code `0x14` dispatches here through `81:8320`. The handler gates on per-player `$119D/$119F`, snapshots race time, decrements `$0EF1/$0EF3` laps, and advances `$1199/$119B`. Run `36801728342` dynamically reproduces identical progression through stock Dragster results. Cross-build correspondence: PAL prototype `81:8050`; Europe retail `81:8050`, independently confirmed by the relocated object-code `0x14` dispatch table. Comparative analyzer evidence shows Europe retail alone contracts the frame-normalization sub-block at `81:8102` from the 22-byte USA/beta/PAL-prototype shape to 8 bytes, after which the handler continues at a -14-byte shift.
SnesPrgRom:8B95:Course_SampleRuntimeSurface:Common per-racer 383-byte routine called from both player paths after collision/contact-shape construction and before later collision handling. It transforms course-relative state and samples both materialized runtime planes at `7E:C000` and `7E:A000/A001`. Full four-ROM structure is proven: PAL prototype and Europe at shift -32; legacy beta byte-identical. Exact meanings of individual surface values remain intentionally open.
SnesPrgRom:A52F:Race_UpdateCameraAndPrepareVramStrips:Race-frame camera update body called through long wrapper `81:A52B` from `83:CD55`. It advances camera position from `$04F5/$04F9`, derives entering-edge/ring coordinates and strip counts, then calls `81:AB88` at `81:A59A` to materialize pending VRAM-strip DMA descriptors.
SnesPrgRom:AA40:Race_FilterVramUpdateListsByCameraWindow:Filters the two compact race VRAM update lists against camera/window edge classes. List A uses count `$0DCD`, destinations `$0D8D+2*i`, selectors `$0D6D+i`, and camera inputs `$0505/$050D/$04F5`; list B mirrors this at `$0DCF/$0DAD/$0D7D` with `$0507/$050F/$04F7`. Matching an edge clears the selector/eligibility byte; destination words remain intact.
SnesPrgRom:AB88:Race_BuildCameraVramDmaDescriptors:Converts camera-derived edge coordinates (`$0505/$0509/$050D/$0511`) and split counts (`$052B/$052F/$0533/$0537`) into up to eight DMA descriptor slots. Each slot records a ready flag, low-WRAM source, byte count, VRAM destination and VMAIN mode in `$03B9/$0399/$03A9/$03C9/$03D9 + 2*n`. Run `36966728136` observes horizontal slot 2 preparing one 32-byte entering column during steady rightward scroll.
SnesPrgRom:B8F1:RNC1_Unpack:Identified by unique opcode-structure match to preserved 1992 SNES `RNC_1.S`; ROM offset `0x00B8F1`. Regional/build equivalents: Europe retail `01:B8E2` (`0x00B8E2`), 1994-11-29 PAL prototype `01:B8D1` (`0x00B8D1`), legacy beta identical to USA.
SnesPrgRom:BB60:RNC1_MakeHuffTail:Preserved Method-1 MAKEHUFF tail begins here; final RTS at `01:BB6E`, establishing the shipped generic RNC1 body end.
SnesPrgRom:BB6F:RNC1_ReadWordLoROMSafe:Integration helper used by the shipped Method-1 decoder for a 16-bit packed-stream read through `IN`. Normal path returns `LDA [IN]` while restoring the pointer; when `IN=$FFFF`, it fetches the high byte from next-bank `$8000`, matching LoROM linear-stream continuity across a bank boundary. `01:BB73` is the helper's wrap-test `BNE`, not a store.
SnesPrgRom:C5AF:HUD_QueueMessage:Long-entry wrapper for `81:C5B3`, the confirmed per-player 32-entry message-ring enqueue routine. P1 uses storage `0CBB+` with indices `0CE1/0CE3`; P2 uses `0CE5+` with `0D0B/0D0D`. Stunt finalization calls this with wipeout/head-bounce/tabletop/trick/praise IDs. Cross-build correspondence: PAL prototype `81:C590`; Europe retail `81:C59C`, independently corroborated by two coherent direct callers.
SnesPrgRom:1007E:APU_UploadBlockById_Wrapper:Exact bytes `JSR $8082; RTL`. Callers load X with audio block IDs before entering; the body at `02:8082` performs the SPC handshake and calls `APU_ResolveBlockPointer` at `02:812A`, establishing X as a block-record selector.
SnesPrgRom:1012A:APU_ResolveBlockPointer:Exact static routine maps block ID X onto the packed audio-record pool beginning at `10:8000`. It walks X length-prefixed records, performing LoROM bank-wrap arithmetic, and returns the selected record pointer in DP `$63-$65`. Package-table IDs `$00-$31` address the first 50 records, but direct setup calls pass IDs through at least `$42`; the same contiguous parser resolves records `$32-$42`. Therefore `$00-$31` is the package-table ID universe, not the resolver's upper bound.
SnesPrgRom:109B9:Race_UpdateRacersFrame:Frame-level racer orchestration. It marshals player 1 persistent state/input into the shared `$0Fxx` current-player workspace, runs collision/stunt/physics helpers including `Stunt_FinalizeAndScoreAirTricks` and `Player_ApplyVerticalAcceleration`, writes results back to player-1 slots, then conditionally repeats the same pipeline for player 2 before returning at `82:9384`.
SnesPrgRom:11A42:Stunt_FinalizeAndScoreAirTricks:Nitrodon's focused `stunts.txt` plus bank-82 listing expose the complete landing/finalization pipeline: roll/flip quarter-turn accumulation, half-twist conversion, Z-flip/tabletop handling, wipeout/headbounce checks, trick-message selection, stunt-combination indexing, praise lookup, and reset of transient stunt state.
SnesPrgRom:12968:Player_ApplyVerticalAcceleration:Nitrodon's bank-82 annotation is directly supported by the instruction flow: current-player Y velocity is capped below `0x0200`, upward motion receives +19, downward acceleration is `19 - Yvel/32`, the result is added to Y velocity, and current-player Y coordinate is incremented. Structural equivalents: PAL prototype `82:A959` (strong, 91.2% byte similarity); Europe retail `82:A96F` (supported, 89.7%), with projected operands following established regional WRAM-motion families.
SnesPrgRom:12A6E:Input_DecodePlayer1Buttons:Nitrodon's bank-82 listing shows raw controller words `$030D/$030F` decoded into explicit A/X/L/R/B/Y/select/start flags and normalized vertical/horizontal direction fields at `$0311/$0315`. Structural equivalents: PAL prototype `82:AA5F` (strong, 98.8% byte similarity) and Europe retail `82:AA75` (strong, 84.6%); Europe coherently shifts the controller-state block by +4 (`030D→0311` through `0335→0339`).
SnesPrgRom:12CA5:Race_BuildRacerOAMState:Per-frame racer sprite/OAM composition, long-entry wrapper at `82:ACA1`, called from the race loop at `83:CD77` after racer simulation/state fetch. It projects both racers relative to camera/viewport bounds, writes screen-coordinate and sprite-attribute state under `$1501-$1510`, and constructs high-OAM control bytes at `7E:20A2/20A3`; later `82:D2xx-D34F` code commits assembled OAM data to `$2104`.
SnesPrgRom:13180:PPU_LoadPaletteAssetToCGRAM:Writes the caller-supplied CGRAM start index X to `$2121`, resolves asset A through the five-byte table rooted at `82:B32F`, and streams the resolved payload to `$2122`. Race setup uses this path for racer palette assets `0x06 + selector`; the representative 2P fixture resolves exact assets `0x06/0x07`.
SnesPrgRom:131D8:PPU_LoadAssetToVRAM:Resolves asset A through the five-byte table at `82:B32F` and streams or dispatches the resource into caller-selected VRAM destination X. Race presentation setup at `82:E02E` uses this path for exact OBJ graphics assets `0x7F` → VRAM `$0000` and `0x80` → `$1000`; both are raw in the USA ROM and round-trip byte-for-byte as 75 and 30 native 4bpp tiles.
SnesPrgRom:1519B:PPU_ConsumeCameraVramDmaDescriptors:NMI-side consumer reached from `80:87E1 -> 82:D197`. It scans the camera-strip descriptor ready flags in reverse slot order, clears each active flag, programs `$2116`, DMA source `$4302`, size `$4305` and `$2115`, then triggers channel 0 through `$420B` to B-bus `$2118`. In the retained Dragster scroll, steady horizontal descriptors are consumed in the same guest frame in which `81:AB88` builds them.
SnesPrgRom:1537F:PPU_EmitFilteredVramUpdateLists:Direct hardware consumer for the two filtered update lists. Iterates each count in reverse, writes destination words from `$0D8D/$0DAD` to `$2116`, indexes the 16-bit data table at `7E:2132` from selector bytes `$0D6D/$0D7D`, applies `XBA`, and stores the result through `$2118`. This is the presentation-emission seam, not gameplay activation.
SnesPrgRom:1602E:Race_LoadPresentationAssets:Race presentation setup for the proven racer family. Loads OBJ graphics resources `0x7F/0x80` to VRAM `$0000/$1000`, other race graphics resources to `$2000/$7000`, shared palettes, and player-colour palette assets `0x06 + selector` into OBJ CGRAM slots. Exact racer graphics/palette provenance is retained in `analysis/generated/racer-presentation-family.json`.
SnesPrgRom:16165:Course_LoadAndMaterialize:Course-load orchestration called from race setup at `82:DE0F`. It selects the course resource from `77:074A`, invokes the generic resource loader with destination bank `$7F` so the RNC path decodes into `7F:0000`, then parses that payload into VRAM and runtime structures including `7E:A000` and `7E:C000`. This matches the independently verified active decoded-course payload.
SnesPrgRom:18971:Text_HandleFCPositionControl:Bounded static analysis of the Sayans translation-control grammar. The routine preserves A, recognizes low-byte `FC`, consumes the following parameter, scans subsequent character bytes until `FF` or `FB`, consults `Text_TestCharacterMetadataBit7` for ordinary bytes, then computes `parameter * 32 + horizontal adjustment` into DP `$9F`. The 32-column arithmetic and translated `FF FC xx` / `FB FC xx` sequences support a centered tilemap-position/layout role. Exact user-facing meaning of the parameter remains unpromoted.
SnesPrgRom:18AF7:CartridgeMemorySelfTest:Called during startup at `80:886D`. It probes mirrored/available memory through `77:0000/1FFF`; success restores the original byte and `RTL`s, while failure deliberately tail-jumps to `80:94EB`.
SnesPrgRom:1F296:RacerPresentation_ResolveFramePointer:Maps a 16-bit racer presentation ID to the three-byte table at `20:8000 + id*3`; the first two bytes are the source address and the third is added to bank base `0x23`. Exact ROM-backed round trips for fixture IDs `0540/0542/0544/057E` are recorded in `analysis/generated/racer-presentation-family.json`.
SnesPrgRom:1F2BB:RacerPresentation_DecodePackedFramePair:Consumes frame pointers resolved by `83:F296`, interprets mask/header fields and packed 16-bit words, and feeds the race-render tile/presentation construction path. Remaining packed-word meanings are intentionally left for on-demand renderer work.
SnesWorkRam:000E:Frontend_SelectedRow:Recovered bot label. On PLAYER_SELECT_P1 the reference harness steps it `0x00..0x07` down the eight rider rows; confirming row r selects rider `2r + column`. Exact semantics outside these menus not yet tested.
SnesWorkRam:009B:Frontend_SelectedOption:Recovered bot uses it for main-menu/tour selection. Native captures show `0x00` at clean main menu and first tours page, consistent with bot policy. On TOUR_SELECT it holds the medal-matrix tour row (Crawler 0, Jumper 1, Shuffler 2 … Hunter 8). Down steps it by 2 because even rows are the left column; confirmed on page `0x6D` (`00→02`) and on the nine-tour page `0x10` (`00→08`, HUNTER).
SnesWorkRam:009F:Frontend_CurrentMenu:Stable deterministic checkpoints correlate `0xD7` main menu, `0x3C` one-player rider select, `0x6D` first one-player tours page, `0xF6` one-player track select, `0x16` now-playing, then `0x00` after race entry. Do not treat it as continuously semantic: bank-80 text/layout handlers also reuse DP `$9F` as a tile/text position accumulator during frontend construction.
SnesWorkRam:00D0:Frontend_SelectedTourRow:Tour row used by `83:9EB4` medal indexing (`16 * D0 + rider`). The tier probe reads `0` for Crawler, `2` for Shuffler and `8` for Hunter on TRACK_SELECT after confirming the matching `$009B`.
SnesWorkRam:017D:P1_RiderIndex:Player-one rider identity 0..15, written on PLAYER_SELECT_P1 confirm as `2 * row + column` (confirmed for MIKE 0, ANDREW 1, MARTIN 2, AMY 4, STEVE 15). It is the medal-matrix column and selects in-race palette asset `0x06 + index`; `$017F` is the P2 counterpart. Preset table: `analysis/generated/legacy-cast-presets.json`.
SnesWorkRam:0302:Race_LoopParityPhase:Race-loop code at `83:CC94-CC9A` computes `1 - $0302` and stores it back every loop iteration, so the byte alternates `0/1`. Racer-update branches at `82:8C3B` (P1) and `82:9122` (P2), plus common physics at `82:A39C/A3A4`, use this parity to choose alternating work. This is the former fixture label `phase2`.
SnesWorkRam:0304:Race_LoopModulo3Phase:Race-loop code at `83:CC87-CC91` increments the byte and wraps `3 -> 0` every loop iteration. This is the former fixture label `phase3`; use it with `$0302` when diagnosing intra-frame race-loop alignment.
SnesWorkRam:0313:Race_ActiveState:Recovered bot label `inRace`; verified as `0x01` after deterministic race entry in both native and Snes9x/snesref runs. Non-race screens can contain other transient values, so only `0x01 = active race` is currently asserted.
SnesWorkRam:033B:Player1_FacingAtTakeoff:Nitrodon's map says this records whether the racer was facing right when leaving the ground. The stunt finalizer uses it to decide whether accumulated quarter-turn direction maps to flip versus roll; paired player-2 slot is `$033D`.
SnesWorkRam:0399:CameraVramDmaSource:Low-WRAM source addresses for camera-strip DMA descriptors. In the representative single-view horizontal path, slot 2 uses `$0433`.
SnesWorkRam:03A9:CameraVramDmaSize:Byte counts for camera-strip DMA descriptors. Steady horizontal slot 2 uses `$0020`, one 16-word column; vertical row descriptors use count-derived sizes such as `$0022`.
SnesWorkRam:03B9:CameraVramDmaReady:Ready flags for the camera-derived VRAM-strip descriptor queue. `81:ACB1` sets the selected slot to 1; NMI consumer `82:D19B..D2D0` clears the flag immediately before emission.
SnesWorkRam:03C9:CameraVramDmaDestination:VRAM destinations for camera-strip DMA descriptors. For the single-view horizontal slot, `81:AB88` stores `$0C00 + $0505`; run `36966728136` observes the 32-column ring wrap `$0D9F -> $0D80`.
SnesWorkRam:03D9:CameraVramDmaVmain:VMAIN modes for camera-strip DMA descriptors. Horizontal column slot 2 uses `$81` (VRAM increment 32 after the high-byte write); the complementary row path uses `$80`.
SnesWorkRam:0411:Player1_XPosition:Effective recovered-bot word key `xPos`. Race-acceleration run 36512546762 moves it identically in native/reference from 1088 at rest to 1655 under staged Right input, alongside positive X speed.
SnesWorkRam:0413:Player2_XPosition:`Race_UpdateRacersFrame` copies this into the same DP/current-player X-position slot used by verified P1 `$0411` and writes the simulated value back after the P2 pass; recovered Dessyreqt tools independently use the same address. Cross-build equivalents recovered from the same bidirectional relation: PAL prototype `7E:0413`; Europe retail `7E:0417`.
SnesWorkRam:0415:Player1_YPosition:Effective recovered-bot word key `yPos`. Sustained-B run 36514981164 causally changes player 1 from ground Y=859 in the matched no-B control to Y=797 while airborne; native and Snes9x match exactly.
SnesWorkRam:0417:Player2_YPosition:`Race_UpdateRacersFrame` copies this into the same DP/current-player Y-position slot used by verified P1 `$0415` and writes the simulated value back after the P2 pass; recovered Dessyreqt tools independently use the same address. Cross-build equivalents: PAL prototype `7E:0417`; Europe retail `7E:041B`.
SnesWorkRam:042B:Player1_ZFlipCount:Nitrodon's map identifies the player-specific slot as Z-flip count. Bank-82 code reads/writes it under 16-bit accumulator mode, caps the landing result at 4, and uses it directly in the stunt-combination index. USJO v8 reads only the low byte.
SnesWorkRam:042D:Player2_ZFlipCount:Nitrodon's WRAM map identifies the paired player-2 Z-flip field; Dessyreqt's `Races2.wch` independently labels it Z-Flips.
SnesWorkRam:042F:Player1_TabletopDuration:Nitrodon's map and annotated bank-82 code identify this as tabletop duration; `INC $042F,X` at `02:9582` advances it and `02:9C87` requires duration >= 3 to emit the Tabletop message. Dynamic run 36776825024 independently observes X input driving low-byte `0→1→2→3→4→0` at a two-frame cadence in native and pinned Snes9x. This supersedes the older accumulated-count label.
SnesWorkRam:0431:Player2_TabletopDuration:Nitrodon's WRAM map identifies player-2 tabletop duration here; Dessyreqt's Tabletop bot and `Races2.wch` independently use the same paired address.
SnesWorkRam:04B7:Player1_XSpeed:Effective recovered-bot word key `xSpeed`; bot explicitly sign-converts it. Race-acceleration run 36512546762 changes it identically from 0 to +447 under Right input while X position increases. Cross-build equivalents proven by racer-update dataflow: PAL prototype `7E:04B7`; Europe retail `7E:04BB`.
SnesWorkRam:04B9:Player2_XSpeed:`Race_UpdateRacersFrame` maps this to shared `CurrentPlayer_XVelocityWorking` `$0F9F` before the P2 simulation pass and writes it back afterward; Dessyreqt independently labels the same field. Cross-build equivalents recovered from the same relation: PAL prototype `7E:04B9`; Europe retail `7E:04BD`.
SnesWorkRam:04BB:Player1_YSpeed:Effective recovered-bot word key `ySpeed`; bot sign-converts it. In run 36514981164 sustained B changes it causally from 0 in the matched control to -21 while player 1 is airborne; native and Snes9x match exactly. Cross-build equivalents proven by racer-update dataflow: PAL prototype `7E:04BB`; Europe retail `7E:04BF`.
SnesWorkRam:04BD:Player2_YSpeed:`Race_UpdateRacersFrame` maps this to shared `CurrentPlayer_YVelocityWorking` `$0FA1` before the P2 simulation pass and writes it back afterward; Dessyreqt independently labels the same field. Cross-build equivalents: PAL prototype `7E:04BD`; Europe retail `7E:04C1`.
SnesWorkRam:04C7:Player1_PitchAngle:Persistent player-1 pitch/orientation. From control angle `0x07`, eight L frames produce `0x37` (= 7−16 mod 64) and eight R frames produce `0x17` (= 7+16 mod 64), identically native/reference. ROM `02:8D84` copies working `$0F49` into this slot.
SnesWorkRam:04C9:Player2_PitchAngle:Paired player-2 pitch/orientation slot. Sibling ROM routine at `02:9272` executes `LDY $0F49; STY $04C9`; surrounding destinations shift coherently to player-2 fields.
SnesWorkRam:04F5:Player1_CameraXSpeed:Nitrodon's map calls this player-1 camera X velocity; Dessyreqt's `Races.wch` and SCUM independently use it as Camera X Speed. SCUM sign-converts the byte.
SnesWorkRam:04F7:Player2_CameraXSpeed:Dessyreqt's `Races2.wch` identifies this paired player-2 Camera X Speed slot. Pairing with corroborated player-1 `$04F5` is strong, but direct runtime validation is still open.
SnesWorkRam:0545:Player1_AirTime:Dynamic run 36514981164 causally changes the low byte from 0 to 9 under sustained B while Y position/speed enter the airborne trajectory. Nitrodon's WRAM map identifies the slot as time in air with a 9-frame maximum, and bank-82 stunt code reads it as a word (`LDA $0545,Y`). Historical bots read only the low byte.
SnesWorkRam:0547:Player2_AirTime:`Race_UpdateRacersFrame` copies this into shared current-player air-time `$0F29` for the P2 pass, exactly parallel to verified P1 `$0545`; the common stunt pipeline consumes the shared value.
SnesWorkRam:0BA1:Player1_FacedDirection:`Race_UpdateRacersFrame` copies this into shared `$0F47` for the P1 pass and writes it back afterward; recovered bot semantics give 1 = right, 0 = left. Exact value convention still lacks an isolated causal test.
SnesWorkRam:0BA3:Player2_FacedDirection:`Race_UpdateRacersFrame` copies this into the same shared `$0F47` slot used by P1 facing and writes it back after the P2 pass; Nitrodon and Dessyreqt independently identify the field.
SnesWorkRam:0C63:Frontend_SelectedColumn:Recovered bot label; native captures change with frontend scene/selection. On PLAYER_SELECT_P1 `0x06` is the left column and `0x07` the right; other screens reuse it (e.g. `0xFD` after confirm).
SnesWorkRam:0CBB:Player1_MessageQueue:`81:C5B3` appends P1 messages at index `0CE3` with 5-bit wrap; `0CE1` is the opposing queue index. Dessyreqt v14a independently parses this ring for pending stunt credit.
SnesWorkRam:0CE1:Player1_MessageQueueReadIndex:Opposing P1 ring index used by enqueue/full checks and historical v14a queue traversal. Exact consumer naming should be verified at the dequeue site.
SnesWorkRam:0CE3:Player1_MessageQueueWriteIndex:`81:C5B3` writes at this index, then increments it modulo 32.
SnesWorkRam:0CE5:Player2_MessageQueue:P2 ring used by the same enqueue routine; write index `0D0D`, opposing index `0D0B`.
SnesWorkRam:0D0D:Player2_MessageQueueWriteIndex:P2 enqueue index; incremented modulo 32 by `81:C5B3`.
SnesWorkRam:0DFD:Player1_ZRotationState:Static scan run 36769525945 exposes paired copy structure: `02:8AB2` loads `$0DFD` and stores it to shared working `$0F57`, while `02:8FBA` loads paired `$0DFF` into the same working slot and `02:8DB2` copies `$0F57` back to `$0DFD`. This strongly supports `$0DFD/$0DFF` as paired persistent racer Z-state slots; exact physical interpretation remains open.
SnesWorkRam:0E0F:RaceTimer_MinutesDigit:Shared race/stunt timer minutes digit. Count-up path `81:C74A-C74E` increments it; stunt countdown `81:C83A-C83E` decrements it. Finish handling snapshots it for the active player at `81:80BE-C1`.
SnesWorkRam:0E13:RaceTimer_TensSecondsDigit:Timer tens-of-seconds digit, range 0-5 in normal operation. Count-up carries here at `81:C738-C747`; countdown borrows through it at `81:C82B-C837`.
SnesWorkRam:0E17:RaceTimer_SecondsDigit:Timer ones-of-seconds digit, range 0-9. Count-up and countdown mutate it at `81:C723-C735` and `81:C819-C828`; finish handling includes it in the active-player time snapshot.
SnesWorkRam:0E1B:RaceTimer_TenthsDigit:Timer tenths digit, range 0-9. The count-up path increments it after six sub-ticks; stunt countdown decrements it after six sub-ticks.
SnesWorkRam:0E1F:RaceTimer_SubTick:Six-step timer phase. Both timer directions advance this field and roll it at 6 before changing the tenths digit, giving the timer its 1/10-second cadence.
SnesWorkRam:0E35:Player_LineCrossingTime:Per-player lap/finish line snapshot written by `Race_HandleCheckpointFinish` (`81:80B6..`): hundredths `0E35,Y` (synthesized: sub-tick mapped 0,2,4,6,6,8 plus `$0300`, capped at 9), minutes `0E39,Y`, tens `0E3D,Y`, seconds `0E41,Y`, tenths `0E45,Y`; `Y` = 2 × player. The write that leaves `0EF1,Y` laps at 0 is the official finish the stock results table prints; the shared `0E0F..` timer keeps running afterwards (R-2026-10-08-UI-01). Zeroed at race entry.
SnesWorkRam:0EF1:Player1_LapsRemaining:Nitrodon's map identifies laps remaining; paired player-2 slot is `$0EF3`.
SnesWorkRam:0F09:CurrentPlayer_CollisionContactWord:Collision/contact result consumed by the course-object dispatcher. `81:82E6` computes C000 index `(($0F09 & $03F0) >> 2) + (($0F09 & $000F) >> 1)`. The collision resolver clears and reconstructs the value from gathered candidates; racer marshaling persists it through `$0E95/$0E97`. This is the gameplay activation boundary for the representative checkpoint/finish object family, distinct from camera-driven presentation preparation.
SnesWorkRam:0F49:CurrentPlayer_PitchAngleWorking:Shared current-player pitch/orientation workspace. The player-state marshal loads it from persistent P1 `04C7` or P2 `04C9`; stunt finalization reads it to track quadrant transitions.
SnesWorkRam:0F49:CurrentPlayer_PitchScratch:Historical bot reads this as `pitch`, but static sibling routines copy the same working value into persistent player-1 `$04C7` or player-2 `$04C9` slots. L-vs-control checkpoints leave `$0F49` unchanged while `$04C7` changes causally, so it should not be treated as stable player-1 storage.
SnesWorkRam:0F4B:CurrentPlayer_AngularVelocityWorking:Shared current-player angular-velocity workspace. The bank-81 player-state bridge round-trips persistent `0BAD/0BAF` through this slot for the common simulation pass. Distinct from `0F4D`, which is a separate per-frame rotation delta used by stunt logic.
SnesWorkRam:0F57:CurrentPlayer_ZRotationWorking:Static scan run 36769525945 shows `$0F57` as shared current-player working state: `02:8AB2` copies player-1 `$0DFD` into it, `02:8FBA` copies paired `$0DFF` into it, `02:8DB2` writes it back to `$0DFD`, and later bank-02 code mutates/clears it. USJO v8 calls the low byte `zprerotation`; exact physical semantics remain open.
SnesWorkRam:0F61:CurrentPlayer_HalfTwistCount:Nitrodon's map identifies this as the current-player number of half-twists. The stunt finalizer at `02:9C2E` shifts it right once to derive full twists, caps the result at 4, and feeds that into the stunt-combination index. Writer analysis independently shows current-player copy/clear/increment behavior.
SnesWorkRam:0F97:CurrentPlayer_RacerPresentationId:Shared current-player presentation ID. Player 1 copies `0FE9→0F97→0FE9`; player 2 copies `0FEB→0F97→0FEB`; `83:ED2F` updates the working identity during the common racer pass.
SnesWorkRam:0F9F:CurrentPlayer_XVelocityWorking:Nitrodon's map identifies current-player X velocity here. Bank-81 collision/transform code reads `$0F9F/$0FA1`, transforms both through matrix terms, and stores the new X/Y pair back; bank-82 movement and boost paths also mutate `$0F9F`. This resolves USJO's old “alternative speed” comment as shared current-player working state rather than a second player-1 field.
SnesWorkRam:0FA1:CurrentPlayer_YVelocityWorking:Nitrodon's map and bank-81 velocity transform pair this with `$0F9F`; bank-82 vertical-acceleration code at `02:A968` updates this slot directly.
SnesWorkRam:0FCB:Player1_ArrowDirection:Effective duplicate-key address used by recovered bot; 0 = right, 1 = left.
SnesWorkRam:0FCC:Player1_ArrowsVisible:Effective duplicate-key address used by recovered bot; 0 means direction arrows showing.
SnesWorkRam:0FEF:CurrentPlayer_Index:`Race_UpdateRacersFrame` explicitly stores `0` before the P1 simulation pass at `82:8C22-8C24` and `2` before the P2 pass at `82:910C-910E`; stunt/movement code then uses it to index paired player slots.
SnesWorkRam:1199:Player1_NextCheckpoint:Nitrodon's map identifies the next-checkpoint field; paired player-2 slot is `$119B`. High-value race-finish fixture target.
SnesWorkRam:119B:Player2_NextCheckpoint:Nitrodon's WRAM map and Dessyreqt's `Races2.wch` independently identify the paired player-2 checkpoint slot.
SnesWorkRam:119D:Player1_FinishGateState:Nitrodon's map records `0` when player 1 can cross the finish line; paired player-2 slot is `$119F`. Exact nonzero state meanings remain open.
SnesWorkRam:11BA:Race_CountdownTimer:Recovered bot word key shared by players. In run 36512546762 it decreases identically in native/reference by exactly `0x0100` per guest frame across the staged pre-start/acceleration checkpoints. Exact game-facing units/threshold meanings remain to be named.
SnesWorkRam:11CD:CurrentPlayer_BoostMeterWorking:`Race_UpdateRacersFrame` proves this is the shared boost workspace: P1 `11CF → 11CD` at `82:8BA2-8BA5` and `11CD → 11CF` at `82:8CF1-8CF4`; P2 `11D1 → 11CD` at `82:8EB1-8EB4` and `11CD → 11D1` at `82:91D9-91DC`. Movement/stunt code then mutates the working value.
SnesWorkRam:11CF:Player1_BoostMeter:Persistent P1 boost slot. `Race_UpdateRacersFrame` copies `11CF → 11CD` before the common P1 simulation pass and writes `11CD → 11CF` afterward. Independently, the P1 message consumer at `81:C167-C17D` applies nonnegative per-message rewards from the signed word table at `81:C4AA` directly to `11CF`, closing the delayed stunt-reward path. Cross-build equivalents: PAL prototype `7E:11D3` ↔ working `7E:11D1`; Europe retail `7E:11D9` ↔ working `7E:11D7`.
SnesWorkRam:11D1:Player2_BoostMeter:Persistent P2 boost slot. `Race_UpdateRacersFrame` copies `11D1 → 11CD` before the common P2 simulation pass and writes `11CD → 11D1` afterward. The mirrored P2 message consumer at `81:C2AA-C2BF` applies the same nonnegative per-message reward table directly to `11D1`. Cross-build equivalents: PAL prototype `7E:11D5`; Europe retail `7E:11DB`, each recovered as the unique second boost slot paired with that build's shared boost workspace.
SnesWorkRam:11F9:Player1_RollCount:Nitrodon's map explicitly records a 2-byte player-1 roll count. The stunt routine increments it with 16-bit `INC $11F9,X` after four quarter-turn progress steps, and may credit a 3/4 roll on landing. USJO v8 reads only the low byte.
SnesWorkRam:11FB:Player2_RollCount:Nitrodon's WRAM map identifies a two-byte player-2 roll count; Dessyreqt's `Races2.wch` independently labels the same address Rolls.
SnesWorkRam:11FD:Player1_FlipCount:Nitrodon's map explicitly records a 2-byte player-1 flip count. The stunt routine increments it with 16-bit `INC $11FD,X` after four quarter-turn progress steps, and may credit a 3/4 flip on landing. USJO v8 reads only the low byte.
SnesWorkRam:11FF:Player2_FlipCount:Nitrodon's WRAM map identifies a two-byte player-2 flip count; Dessyreqt's `Races2.wch` independently labels the same address Flips.
SnesWorkRam:1201:Player1_RollQuarterProgress:Bank-82 stunt code increments this progress value and converts it into one roll when it reaches 4, then resets it; paired player-2 slot is `$1203`.
SnesWorkRam:1205:Player1_FlipQuarterProgress:Bank-82 stunt code increments this progress value and converts it into one flip when it reaches 4, then resets it; paired player-2 slot is `$1207`.
SnesWorkRam:12AF:Player1_StuntScoreDisplayCache:Bank 81 proves this is a display-side cache rather than the score accumulator: `81:C37F` loads backing score `77:07BB`, `C383` compares it with `12AF`, `C388` stores a changed value, then `C38B-C3E7` decomposes it into decimal display digits. P1 queue consumption mutates `77:07BB` directly.
SnesWorkRam:132B:Race_ReverseControls:Recovered bot: 2 = controls reversed, 0 = normal. Not yet independently validated.
SnesWorkRam:1361:Player1_StuntAirLatch:Olivier retained this as an “Air flag”; Nitrodon's stunt disassembly shows `02:9A97` setting indexed `$1361` to 1 during stunt-air initialization and `02:9D88` clearing it during finalization. Exact relationship to ordinary air-time `$0545` remains open.
SnesWorkRam:1509:Player1_ScreenX:`Race_BuildRacerOAMState` computes this from P1 world X `0411` relative to camera X `0419`, after viewport scaling/culling and wrap masking. Offscreen fallback stores `0x70`. This corrects the older generic TAS label “Screen X Position.”
SnesWorkRam:150A:Player1_ScreenY:`Race_BuildRacerOAMState` stores projected P1 Y here from `0415 - 041D`; offscreen fallback stores `0x70`.
SnesWorkRam:150D:Player2_ScreenX:P2 counterpart of `1509`, projected from `0413` relative to camera `0419` through the same horizontal policy.
SnesWorkRam:150E:Player2_ScreenY:P2 counterpart of `150A`, projected from `0417 - 041D`; offscreen fallback stores `0x70`.
SnesWorkRam:C000:Course_RuntimeObjectMap:Authoritative course behavior plane materialized during course load. `81:82E6` derives an index from `CurrentPlayer_CollisionContactWord` (`$0F09`), reads an object byte, clears bit 0, and dispatches through a bounded behavior jump table. Runtime object code `0x14` selects the confirmed checkpoint/finish handler. Camera position and the `$0DCD/$0DCF` VRAM-update lists do not participate in this activation decision. Exact dimensions/layout remain to be mapped.
SnesWorkRam:10000:Course_ActiveDecodedPayload:Confirmed by run 36514985916: active Dragster decoded stream 1 matches 33,814/33,815 bytes from this address; only decoded byte 11 is runtime-mutated.
SnesWorkRam:1000B:Course_ResourceListCursor:`Course_LoadAndMaterialize` reads this word as an offset from `7F:0000`, increments it once per resource ID consumed, and stops on `FF`. Dragster initializes it to `0x840F` and finishes at `0x8416`, exactly explaining the historical `0x0F→0x16` low-byte mutation.
